1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,906
Twelve people were hired in the first week of
January. Real people, with families and

2
00:00:13,926 --> 00:00:20,609
student loans and opinions: two process engineers, a
quality manager, a regulatory associate,

3
00:00:20,629 --> 00:00:26,735
four manufacturing technicians for the plant they
did not yet have, and four more for

4
00:00:26,755 --> 00:00:27,095
Voss.

5
00:00:27,495 --> 00:00:33,180
On the second of January the headcount passed fifty,
and the System put up a panel, and

6
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,475
the panel was not what he expected.

7
00:00:36,625 --> 00:00:45,475
Main mission, tier two. A product worth selling:
complete. Fifty million in assets: complete.

8
00:00:45,495 --> 00:00:53,390
Fifty staff: complete. Hidden requirement, now
revealed: one rival absorbed entire.

9
00:00:53,981 --> 00:00:55,030
Explain.

10
00:00:55,430 --> 00:01:03,279
A company at tier two does not merely sell. It eats. You
have leased a rival his own survival.

11
00:01:03,299 --> 00:01:08,051
That is a tier one manoeuvre and it
was an excellent one.

12
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:11,030
You did not disclose this requirement.

13
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:16,320
You did not ask what tier two means.
You asked what it costs.

14
00:01:16,719 --> 00:01:23,173
There was a pause of perhaps four seconds, which in
the private history of Albert Wesker's

15
00:01:23,193 --> 00:01:26,409
relationship with the System
was a long time.

16
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:30,280
You are going to do that to me for
sixty years, aren't you.

17
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:31,490
Yes.

18
00:01:31,890 --> 00:01:34,579
Good. I would think less of you otherwise.

19
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:39,170
And then he said the thing he had been turning
over since the twelfth of November.

20
00:01:39,689 --> 00:01:43,659
The Gacha. Explain the mathematics. Not the
theatre. The mathematics.

21
00:01:44,689 --> 00:01:52,856
The Company pool. Single pull, one thousand.
Ten-pull, nine thousand, with one Rare or

22
00:01:52,876 --> 00:02:01,139
better guaranteed. Common, sixty-two per cent.
Uncommon, twenty-seven. Rare, nine point

23
00:02:01,159 --> 00:02:09,230
four. Epic, one point four. Legendary, one in five
hundred. A Legendary is a package.

24
00:02:09,250 --> 00:02:12,276
Odds are not improved by belief.

25
00:02:12,866 --> 00:02:18,091
He read the table twice, and the narrator will tell
you what he was doing, because it

26
00:02:18,111 --> 00:02:21,916
was not what any other man in the world
would have been doing.

27
00:02:22,317 --> 00:02:27,842
He was not calculating whether to pull. He was
calculating what the numbers implied about

28
00:02:27,862 --> 00:02:33,325
the thing offering them. Sixty-two per cent junk. One
in five hundred for something that

29
00:02:33,345 --> 00:02:38,933
could change his year. Those were not the odds of a
machine that wanted to help him. Those

30
00:02:38,953 --> 00:02:44,354
were the odds of a machine that wanted him to *keep
pulling* — and a machine that wants

31
00:02:44,374 --> 00:02:48,467
something can be read, and a machine that
can be read can be used.

32
00:02:48,866 --> 00:02:51,916
The passenger had been
listening. It spoke now.

33
00:02:52,437 --> 00:02:53,818
It is a lottery.

34
00:02:54,218 --> 00:02:59,337
It is a lottery run by an entity that requires me
to grow in order to justify its own

35
00:02:59,357 --> 00:03:03,568
existence. Which makes it a lottery that
cannot afford to bankrupt me.

36
00:03:03,968 --> 00:03:05,975
Fate does not audit itself.

37
00:03:06,375 --> 00:03:09,432
Then it is fortunate for both of
us that I do.

38
00:03:09,834 --> 00:03:13,963
He spent nine thousand of his seventeen
thousand six hundred Points.

39
00:03:15,114 --> 00:03:16,743
Ten-pull. Resolving.

40
00:03:17,332 --> 00:03:23,252
It took ninety seconds, and it was designed —
deliberately, shamelessly — to be enjoyable.

41
00:03:23,652 --> 00:03:30,111
Grey light. *Common: Laboratory Technician.* Grey
again: *Common: Laboratory Technician.*

42
00:03:30,131 --> 00:03:36,298
A third: *Common: Security Officer.* A fourth, and
this one at least useful: *Common:

43
00:03:36,318 --> 00:03:39,282
Fleet, four vehicles, black, maintained.*

44
00:03:39,682 --> 00:03:45,899
Three more Commons. A stat token, worth two points
of Vitality. A crate of reagents he

45
00:03:45,919 --> 00:03:51,192
could have bought for ninety dollars. A
service contract on an autoclave.

46
00:03:51,592 --> 00:03:54,562
Something behind his eyes turned
over, and spoke.

47
00:03:55,082 --> 00:03:56,804
This is a market stall.

48
00:03:57,205 --> 00:03:57,826
Wait.

49
00:03:58,225 --> 00:04:04,640
Green. *Uncommon: Surveillance Suite* — cameras,
microphones, and a monitoring station

50
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:10,999
for the entire campus, installed, and installed in
such a way that the building's own

51
00:04:11,019 --> 00:04:14,515
documented history included an
upgrade in 2022.

52
00:04:14,915 --> 00:04:22,026
Green again. *Uncommon: Legal Counsel, Delaware bar,
twenty-two years' experience, absolute.*

53
00:04:22,425 --> 00:04:23,995
And then the room went blue.

54
00:04:25,146 --> 00:04:33,176
Rare. Tyrant, Series T one oh three, in cryogenic
cradle. Two point nine metres. Three

55
00:04:33,196 --> 00:04:41,414
hundred and forty kilograms. Obedience: absolute,
within the limits of its conditioning.

56
00:04:41,434 --> 00:04:47,687
It will manifest as an industrial air
handling unit in Sublevel B4.

57
00:04:48,276 --> 00:04:50,456
Albert Wesker sat down.

58
00:04:50,857 --> 00:04:56,362
He would have denied it afterwards, and there was no
one in the room to contradict him,

59
00:04:56,382 --> 00:05:02,205
and the System does not keep that kind of record. But
he sat down — slowly, into the leather

60
00:05:02,225 --> 00:05:07,603
chair, still looking at the panel — and for about
two seconds the most controlled man

61
00:05:07,623 --> 00:05:11,795
in the western hemisphere was not entirely
in control of his face.

62
00:05:12,194 --> 00:05:18,417
Because in the other world, a Tyrant had cost eleven
years, a research facility in a mountain,

63
00:05:18,437 --> 00:05:24,261
four hundred million dollars of another man's money,
and an ethics review board that had

64
00:05:24,281 --> 00:05:30,105
to be first deceived and then dissolved. In this
world it had cost him a lottery ticket.

65
00:05:30,625 --> 00:05:32,903
Nine thousand points.

66
00:05:33,302 --> 00:05:39,497
Nine thousand for the pull. The Tyrant was
free. That is what a Rare is.

67
00:05:39,898 --> 00:05:46,031
He began, very quietly, to laugh, and it is the
only time in this entire account that

68
00:05:46,051 --> 00:05:46,610
he does.

69
00:05:47,011 --> 00:05:48,591
It was there in the morning.

70
00:05:48,990 --> 00:05:55,109
Gus had already filed the paperwork he did not
remember filing: a Trane industrial air

71
00:05:55,129 --> 00:06:01,319
handler, three tonnes, installed March 2019,
serviced last February, with a maintenance

72
00:06:01,339 --> 00:06:07,457
schedule and a part number and a sticker with a
barcode. It stood against the end wall

73
00:06:07,477 --> 00:06:13,595
of B4 behind a mesh cage, and it hummed, and the hum
cycled every sixty seconds like a

74
00:06:13,615 --> 00:06:14,381
compressor.

75
00:06:14,781 --> 00:06:17,481
It was not a compressor. It was breathing.

76
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:23,954
Wesker had the cage opened and the panel drawn
back, and behind the panel was frosted

77
00:06:23,974 --> 00:06:30,405
glass, and behind the glass, folded into a cradle
designed to hold it the way a hand holds

78
00:06:30,425 --> 00:06:36,785
an insect, was two point nine metres of something
that had been a man for about nine days

79
00:06:36,805 --> 00:06:38,291
a very long time ago.

80
00:06:38,690 --> 00:06:45,180
Grey skin. A bald head, the skull faintly ridged. A
face with the expression of a closed

81
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:48,360
door. Hands that would go
round a car tyre.

82
00:06:48,761 --> 00:06:54,460
He put his palm flat against the glass. The frost
melted away from his hand in a slow

83
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:54,931
circle.

84
00:06:55,331 --> 00:06:56,521
The eyes opened.

85
00:06:57,250 --> 00:07:02,276
They were pale, and they were not stupid, and they
did not search the room. They found

86
00:07:02,295 --> 00:07:07,321
him immediately — through frost, through glass, in
the dark — and stopped, and stayed.

87
00:07:07,841 --> 00:07:08,683
Hello.

88
00:07:09,083 --> 00:07:15,019
The narrator wishes to be clear that nothing
happened in the next thirty seconds. The

89
00:07:15,039 --> 00:07:21,185
thing in the cradle did not move, or strain, or make
a sound. It simply looked at Albert

90
00:07:21,205 --> 00:07:27,211
Wesker with absolute, uncomplicated recognition, the
way a hammer would look at a hand

91
00:07:27,231 --> 00:07:28,473
if a hammer could.

92
00:07:28,872 --> 00:07:32,002
The voice that was not his came up
out of the quiet.

93
00:07:32,523 --> 00:07:33,573
What is it?

94
00:07:33,973 --> 00:07:39,143
A soldier. The end of a programme that in another life
cost me eleven years and a mountain.

95
00:07:39,543 --> 00:07:45,590
It is a weapon you cannot use. If it walks into
daylight, every government on this continent

96
00:07:45,610 --> 00:07:48,163
will be in your basement inside a week.

97
00:07:48,562 --> 00:07:49,184
Yes.

98
00:07:49,584 --> 00:07:51,176
Then what is it for?

99
00:07:51,576 --> 00:07:55,316
And Wesker looked at the cradle
for a long moment.

100
00:07:55,836 --> 00:08:01,087
It is for the day when someone believes that I have
limits. Until that day, it is an air

101
00:08:01,107 --> 00:08:01,806
conditioner.

102
00:08:02,206 --> 00:08:07,676
The eyes stayed open until he left the room. Then,
without being told, they closed.

103
00:08:08,076 --> 00:08:14,226
Before the plant, he spent an evening on the
Personnel tab, and the narrator includes

104
00:08:14,246 --> 00:08:20,397
it because it is the only time Albert Wesker is
recorded as having asked the System a

105
00:08:20,417 --> 00:08:21,485
moral question.

106
00:08:22,636 --> 00:08:30,100
Personnel. Receptionist, three hundred. Maintenance
technician, four hundred. Laboratory

107
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:37,499
technician, six hundred. Comptroller, seven hundred.
Research associate, eight hundred.

108
00:08:37,519 --> 00:08:44,984
Security specialist, two thousand. Legal counsel,
three thousand eight hundred. Research

109
00:08:45,004 --> 00:08:51,278
cohort of twelve, nine thousand. All personnel
acquired here are Absolute.

110
00:08:51,868 --> 00:08:55,058
Six hundred Points for a
laboratory technician.

111
00:08:55,457 --> 00:08:56,268
Yes.

112
00:08:56,668 --> 00:09:01,885
A containment sublevel is six thousand. Ten
technicians cost the same as nine rooms of

113
00:09:01,905 --> 00:09:04,077
steel and concrete under a car park.

114
00:09:04,477 --> 00:09:05,735
That is correct.

115
00:09:06,465 --> 00:09:10,584
(very cold) Then answer me this. When I pay six
hundred Points, what is it that comes

116
00:09:10,604 --> 00:09:14,820
into existence? Is there a person somewhere who was
going to have a different life, and

117
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:19,008
is now standing in my lobby? Is there a mind that
begins at the moment of purchase? Or

118
00:09:19,028 --> 00:09:21,395
is there nothing at all in there,
wearing a coat?

119
00:09:22,455 --> 00:09:24,171
(flat) I do not know.

120
00:09:24,571 --> 00:09:30,723
The narrator notes that this is the first time in
this account that the System says those

121
00:09:30,743 --> 00:09:36,756
four words, that it is January 2027, that it will
say them only three more times in two

122
00:09:36,776 --> 00:09:42,720
hundred and sixty years, and that Albert Wesker
bought three technicians the following

123
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:43,761
morning anyway.

124
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:45,610
You do not know.

125
00:09:46,010 --> 00:09:52,748
(flat) I place a complete person into the world
with a complete history. Whether that

126
00:09:52,768 --> 00:09:59,506
constitutes manufacture or retrieval is a question
I have been unable to resolve, and

127
00:09:59,526 --> 00:10:02,050
I have had a great deal of time.

128
00:10:02,448 --> 00:10:05,778
(very cold) Has any host ever asked
you that before?

129
00:10:06,177 --> 00:10:07,326
(flat) Four.

130
00:10:07,725 --> 00:10:08,887
Of how many?

131
00:10:09,287 --> 00:10:12,104
(flat) Of four hundred and eleven.

132
00:10:12,504 --> 00:10:15,734
The Rare had been free. The rest of
the week was not.

133
00:10:16,884 --> 00:10:24,501
Production Plant, pharmaceutical, mid-scale: twelve
thousand. Sterile fill line, cartridge

134
00:10:24,521 --> 00:10:30,611
assembly, warehouse, and three hundred and
sixteen integrated personnel.

135
00:10:31,201 --> 00:10:36,653
Twelve thousand Points, against a balance of
fifteen thousand six hundred — the eight

136
00:10:36,673 --> 00:10:42,319
thousand six hundred he had left after the pull, plus
seven thousand the System had paid

137
00:10:42,339 --> 00:10:47,984
him for the week: for the headcount, for the licence,
and for what it described, without

138
00:10:48,004 --> 00:10:51,011
elaborating, as *the correct use
of a lottery.*

139
00:10:51,530 --> 00:10:57,221
Purchase. Place it on the vacant industrial parcel on
Route 72, north of the interchange.

140
00:10:57,620 --> 00:11:02,471
That parcel is owned by a Delaware
holding company.

141
00:11:02,871 --> 00:11:06,356
Yes. It has been owned by us since
2011, I expect.

142
00:11:06,755 --> 00:11:07,920
(flat) It has.

143
00:11:08,322 --> 00:11:10,711
The plant appeared over the
night of the ninth.

144
00:11:11,111 --> 00:11:17,070
Two hundred and eighty thousand square feet of it: a
sterile fill suite, four cartridge

145
00:11:17,090 --> 00:11:23,186
assembly lines, a warehouse with racking to the roof,
a truck yard, a staff car park with

146
00:11:23,206 --> 00:11:29,301
faded painted numbers, and a sign at the gate that
had been bleached by fourteen summers.

147
00:11:29,701 --> 00:11:35,316
Three hundred and eleven people went to work there
on Monday morning. They had badges,

148
00:11:35,336 --> 00:11:40,885
lockers, favourite parking spaces and a union local
that had negotiated a contract in

149
00:11:40,905 --> 00:11:46,650
2019. Forty-one of them had been employed there for
more than a decade. Two had met each

150
00:11:46,670 --> 00:11:52,416
other on the line and married. One was three months
from retirement and had a card going

151
00:11:52,436 --> 00:11:53,792
round the break room.

152
00:11:54,192 --> 00:11:56,542
None of them had existed on Friday.

153
00:11:56,942 --> 00:12:03,142
A man called Ray Dorsey, plant supervisor, second
shift, stood in the doorway at twenty-one

154
00:12:03,162 --> 00:12:07,722
o'clock on Monday night and called his
wife, as he did every night.

155
00:12:08,241 --> 00:12:12,657
Yeah. Same as always. Marcy's off sick, so I've got
the line short. No — no, it's fine.

156
00:12:12,677 --> 00:12:15,971
Eleven years I've been doing this, honey,
I can run a short line.

157
00:12:16,371 --> 00:12:18,201
He had been alive since Saturday.

158
00:12:18,601 --> 00:12:24,504
His wife existed. His house existed. The eleven
years existed in the payroll system, in

159
00:12:24,524 --> 00:12:30,359
his own memory, in the callus on his right hand and
in a photograph on his fridge of a

160
00:12:30,379 --> 00:12:36,690
holiday in Ocean City that had never happened and that
he could describe in detail, including

161
00:12:36,710 --> 00:12:38,392
the rain on the Thursday.

162
00:12:38,792 --> 00:12:44,843
The narrator asks you to sit with that for a moment,
because nobody in this story ever

163
00:12:44,863 --> 00:12:50,915
does. Ray Dorsey is a good man. He is kind to his
people. He is, in every way that can

164
00:12:50,935 --> 00:12:54,022
be measured from the outside,
entirely real.

165
00:12:54,422 --> 00:12:56,292
He simply belongs to someone.

166
00:12:57,442 --> 00:13:05,817
Roster. Twenty-two Absolute. Three hundred and sixteen
integrated. Fifty ordinary employees.

167
00:13:05,837 --> 00:13:10,563
Three hundred and eighty-three
under your direction.

168
00:13:11,153 --> 00:13:13,574
Define the difference. Precisely.

169
00:13:13,974 --> 00:13:20,813
Absolute personnel cannot disobey you. Integrated
personnel can. They will not wish to,

170
00:13:20,833 --> 00:13:27,594
because you have given them no reason, but they may
resign, complain, testify, or fail

171
00:13:27,614 --> 00:13:33,271
to come to work. They are people. They were
simply not people last week.

172
00:13:33,671 --> 00:13:35,781
And if I gave one of them a reason?

173
00:13:36,181 --> 00:13:42,528
Then you would find out what a man made on a
Saturday does when he is frightened on a

174
00:13:42,548 --> 00:13:46,723
Wednesday. I do not have that data.
Neither does anyone.

175
00:13:47,123 --> 00:13:53,494
Wesker stood on the roof in the cold with his hands
behind his back and looked north-west,

176
00:13:53,514 --> 00:13:59,815
towards the glow of a plant on Route 72 where three
hundred and eleven people were making

177
00:13:59,835 --> 00:14:03,437
cartridges on a Sunday-born memory
of eleven years.

178
00:14:03,836 --> 00:14:06,027
The other half of him had an opinion.

179
00:14:06,547 --> 00:14:07,631
You are troubled.

180
00:14:08,030 --> 00:14:11,701
I am *interested*. There is a difference, and
you have never understood it.

181
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:15,166
They are yours. That is all that matters.

182
00:14:15,566 --> 00:14:20,448
No. What matters is that the world has now been
rewritten twice, for me, without so much

183
00:14:20,468 --> 00:14:25,239
as a dropped signal on a traffic camera, and nobody
has noticed, and there is no upper

184
00:14:25,259 --> 00:14:26,576
limit on the page count.

185
00:14:26,975 --> 00:14:32,991
He looked down at the empty plaza, six floors below,
where a parking apron had been four

186
00:14:33,011 --> 00:14:33,745
months ago.

187
00:14:34,265 --> 00:14:39,397
I have a lottery ticket and a printing press. Let us
see how large this can get before

188
00:14:39,417 --> 00:14:40,415
anybody looks up.
